4. Convert TotalCharges into Numeric
df["TotalCharges"] = pd.to_numeric(    df["TotalCharges"], errors="coerce")



- df["TotalCharges"] – Selects the TotalCharges column.
- pd.to_numeric() – Converts values into numerical format.
- errors="coerce" – Converts invalid values into NaN (missing values).
Purpose: To make sure the TotalCharges column contains numerical values.
5. Remove Extra Spaces
for col in df.select_dtypes(include="object").columns:    df[col] = df[col].astype(str).str.strip()



- for col in – Loops through each selected column.
- select_dtypes(include="object") – Selects text or categorical columns.
- .columns – Returns their column names.
- astype(str) – Converts values into strings.
- .str.strip() – Removes extra spaces from the beginning and end of text.
Example: " Male " becomes "Male".
6. Handle Missing Values
for col in df.select_dtypes(include=np.number).columns:    df[col] = df[col].fillna(df[col].median())for col in df.select_dtypes(include="object").columns:    df[col] = df[col].fillna(df[col].mode()[0])



First loop:
- select_dtypes(include=np.number) – Selects numerical columns.
- .median() – Calculates the middle value.
- .fillna() – Replaces missing values with the median.
Second loop:
- select_dtypes(include="object") – Selects categorical columns.
- .mode() – Finds the most frequently occurring value.
- [0] – Selects the first mode.
- .fillna() – Replaces missing values with the most frequent value.
Purpose: To handle missing values in numerical and categorical columns.
7. Remove Duplicate Records
df.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)



- drop_duplicates() – Removes repeated rows.
- inplace=True – Modifies the original dataset directly.
8. Convert Churn into Numeric
df["Churn"] = df["Churn"].replace({"Yes": 1, "No": 0})df["Churn"] = pd.to_numeric(df["Churn"], errors="coerce")df.dropna(subset=["Churn"], inplace=True)



- df["Churn"] – Selects the Churn column.
- .replace() – Replaces Yes with 1 and No with 0.
- pd.to_numeric() – Converts the column into numerical format.
- errors="coerce" – Converts invalid values into NaN.
- dropna(subset=["Churn"]) – Removes rows where Churn is missing.
- inplace=True – Updates the original DataFrame.
Example:
Churn Before	Churn After
Yes	1
No	0
Yes	1

9. Feature Engineering
df["AvgMonthlySpend"] = df["TotalCharges"] / (df["tenure"] + 1)df["LongTermCustomer"] = np.where(df["tenure"] >= 24, 1, 0)



First statement:
- AvgMonthlySpend – Creates a new column.
- TotalCharges – Selects total customer charges.
- tenure – Represents the number of months a customer has stayed.
- +1 – Avoids division by zero.
- / – Divides total charges by tenure plus one.
Second statement:
- np.where() – Applies a condition.
- df["tenure"] >= 24 – Checks whether the customer has stayed for at least 24 months.
- 1 – Assigns 1 if the condition is true.
- 0 – Assigns 0 if the condition is false.
Purpose: Feature engineering creates new useful features from existing data.
10. Handle Outliers Using IQR
numeric_cols = ["tenure", "MonthlyCharges", "TotalCharges"]for col in numeric_cols:    Q1 = df[col].quantile(0.25)    Q3 = df[col].quantile(0.75)    IQR = Q3 - Q1    lower = Q1 - 1.5 * IQR    upper = Q3 + 1.5 * IQR    df[col] = df[col].clip(lower, upper)



- numeric_cols – Stores the numerical column names.
- for col in numeric_cols – Loops through each numerical column.
- quantile(0.25) – Calculates the first quartile (Q1).
- quantile(0.75) – Calculates the third quartile (Q3).
- IQR = Q3 - Q1 – Calculates the interquartile range.
- lower – Calculates the lower limit.
- upper – Calculates the upper limit.
- .clip(lower, upper) – Replaces values below the lower limit with the lower limit and values above the upper limit with the upper limit.
Formula:
\[
IQR = Q3-Q1
\]

\[
Lower = Q1-1.5\times IQR
\]

\[
Upper = Q3+1.5\times IQR
\]

Purpose: To reduce the effect of extreme values in numerical columns.
11. Remove Customer ID
df.drop("customerID", axis=1, inplace=True)



- drop() – Removes a column or row.
- "customerID" – Specifies the column to remove.
- axis=1 – Indicates column removal.
- inplace=True – Updates the original dataset.
Purpose: Customer ID is an identification column and is generally not useful for predicting customer churn.
12. Encode Categorical Columns
categorical_cols = df.select_dtypes(include="object").columnsfor col in categorical_cols:    encoder = LabelEncoder()    df[col] = encoder.fit_transform(df[col])



- categorical_cols – Stores the names of categorical columns.
- select_dtypes(include="object") – Selects text columns.
- for col in categorical_cols – Loops through categorical columns.
- LabelEncoder() – Creates a label encoder.
- fit_transform() – Learns the categories and converts them into numerical values.
- df[col] – Stores the encoded values.
Example:
Gender	Encoded Value
Female	0
Male	1

Purpose: Machine learning algorithms generally require numerical input, so categorical data is converted into numbers.
13. Split Features and Target
X = df.drop("Churn", axis=1)y = df["Churn"]



- X – Stores input features (independent variables).
- df.drop("Churn", axis=1) – Removes the target column from input data.
- y – Stores the target variable.
- df["Churn"] – Selects the Churn column.
Purpose: To separate input data and output data for machine learning.
14. Split Training and Testing Data
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(    X, y, test_size=0.20, random_state=42, stratify=y)



- train_test_split() – Divides data into training and testing sets.
- X – Input features.
- y – Target variable.
- X_train – Training input data.
- X_test – Testing input data.
- y_train – Training output data.
- y_test – Testing output data.
- test_size=0.20 – Uses 20% data for testing and 80% for training.
- random_state=42 – Ensures reproducible splitting.
- stratify=y – Maintains the same target class proportions in training and testing sets.
15. Feature Scaling
scale_cols = [    "tenure", "MonthlyCharges",    "TotalCharges", "AvgMonthlySpend"]scaler = StandardScaler()X_train[scale_cols] = scaler.fit_transform(X_train[scale_cols])X_test[scale_cols] = scaler.transform(X_test[scale_cols])



- scale_cols – Stores the numerical columns that need scaling.
- StandardScaler() – Creates a standard scaling object.
- fit_transform() – Calculates mean and standard deviation from training data and scales it.
- transform() – Applies the same scaling to testing data.
- X_train – Scales training features.
- X_test – Scales testing features.
Purpose: To bring numerical features onto a similar scale.

Formula:
\[
Z=\frac{X-\mu}{\sigma}
\]

Where:
- X = Original value.
- μ = Mean.
- σ = Standard deviation.
- Z = Standardized value.
16. Display Results
print("Training Data Shape:", X_train.shape)print("Testing Data Shape:", X_test.shape)



- X_train.shape – Displays the number of training rows and columns.
- X_test.shape – Displays the number of testing rows and columns.
- print() – Displays the results.
17. Save Cleaned Dataset
df.to_csv("Telecom_Customer_Churn_Cleaned.csv", index=False)print("Cleaned Dataset Saved Successfully!")



- to_csv() – Saves the cleaned DataFrame into a CSV file.
- "Telecom_Customer_Churn_Cleaned.csv" – Specifies the output filename.
- index=False – Does not save the DataFrame index as an extra column.
- print() – Displays the successful saving message.
